WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:15.900
In '72, Millie became
the associate department

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:21.360
head in EECS and before
then and since then

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.920
has had a very illustrious
career in research and science.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.200
At one time, Millie, I
guess about 10 years ago,

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.780
was president of the
American Physical Society.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.910
She's an institute professor
and has appointments

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.560
in both the
department of physics

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.984
and the department of
electrical engineering.

00:00:36.984 --> 00:00:39.120
She won the National
Medal of Science

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.570
back in 1990, probably
the highest award

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.790
that the country US can give
to one of its scientists.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:51.210
She's the recipient of
over 17 honorary degrees.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:54.960
Recently in 1999, she
received an honorary degree

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.150
from the Sorbonne in Paris.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.000
And it turned out that
she's the first woman

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.520
to have received an honorary
degree in the sciences

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.290
from the Sorbonne.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.410
In this year, just last week,
she received an honorary degree

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.720
from the University
of Leuven in Belgium

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:13.260
and next week will be giving
the [? Celsius ?] lectures

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.270
at the University of
Uppsala in Sweden.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:20.250
As I say, Millie has a very
illustrious and long list

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:20.770
of honors.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:22.320
And if I went
through all of them,

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.090
I'd take up half of her time.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.405
I think you really want
to hear from Millie.

00:01:27.405 --> 00:01:30.375
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.840
I'm very honored to
be the first one.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.920
Again, I did the first in
the EECS series about a year

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.390
or a year and a half ago.

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:46.770
And so it's auspicious
beginning, as we all say.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:51.090
My topic today is very
different from last time.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:56.490
This time, I'm talking
about bismuth nanowires.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:00.120
And I could give almost
the same topic title

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.050
as I gave last time,
which was The Remarkable

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.050
Structure of Properties
of Carbon Nanotubes.

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:10.380
That's the last one I did
in the EECS Colloquium.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.510
But I was a little
bit more modest

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.660
because the bismuth
nanowires are a lot newer

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.250
and maybe we don't know exactly
all the remarkable properties

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.530
about them yet.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:27.800
Well, I have a
few collaborators,

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:32.790
actually worldwide, and a
lot of graduate students.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:36.330
And three of them have
already gotten their PhDs

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:43.890
related to this topic, so a good
subject for training students.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:48.300
Now, in light of
the invitation, I

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:54.900
thought I would focus my remarks
about nanostructures before I

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.020
start talking about bismuth.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.650
Those of you who listened to the
president's State of the Union

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:06.060
speech, he didn't have a lot to
say about science, but he did

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.390
have something to say
about nanostructures.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:12.400
And so nanowires are a
part of nanostructures.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.810
So I'd like to
call your attention

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.850
to the importance of this.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:21.240
And in my first
viewgraph, I'm trying

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:26.370
to give you the scale of
what a nanostructure is.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:31.380
And so here we have a
plot, and we have a plot

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.570
of different things on a scale.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:39.900
So you see that the
x-axis here gives size.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.000
And there's a line here.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.170
That's at about 50, 70
nanometers, something

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:47.190
like that.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:53.430
And anything below that begins
to show quantum effects.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:56.070
And with quantum
effects, it's not

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:59.220
that we're just making
things smaller and smaller

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:02.210
to get a higher packing
ratio of whatever component

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:06.060
it is like we're doing
silicon technology.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:10.220
The first thought is to pack
our devices smaller and smaller

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.360
so you get a higher
packing density.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:13.890
But when you do
this, you also get

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:17.430
new physics that comes in that
allows you to do new things.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:19.589
And it's the second
point of view

00:04:19.589 --> 00:04:22.440
that I'd like to stress
in my talk today.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.750
So you see here on
this picture, there

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:26.070
are a lot of different things--

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:31.500
GMR, Giant Magneto Resistance;
quantum dots and lasers;

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.210
nanotubes, carbon
nanotubes, which

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.990
I talked about in my last
EECS Colloquium, et cetera.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:40.320
And bismuth nanowires
span the scale

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:46.260
moving into the
nanostructure regime

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:50.250
where quantum effects
are important.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:55.680
My second viewgraph of a general
nature about nanostructures

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:59.640
tells you in simple
terms why nanostructures

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:04.170
are important and interesting,
why anybody cares about them,

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.470
why President Clinton said
that this is something

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:09.700
that we should be thinking
about and working on.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:11.320
Why is that?

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:14.580
Well, when we have bulk
semiconductors as we know

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.690
and we love in the industry,
what we basically have is this

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:23.910
situation shown on top where
the density of states--

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:26.760
that is the number of
states per energy range--

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:28.530
is essentially continuous.

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:31.620
That is, we have a
crystal structure

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.640
that's really quite large
compared to the quantum regime

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.140
limit.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:41.160
On the other end of the
scale here, quantum dot,

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:44.970
labelled quantum dot, where
we might have a single atom

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:50.010
or molecule, we have
discrete states.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:53.670
And somewhere in between, we
have one-dimensional systems.

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:56.040
Like here, we have
the quantum wire.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.470
Or in this case, we
have the quantum well,

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:00.810
which is a
two-dimensional system.

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:03.690
And you will see that
the density of states--

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:07.080
that is the number of
states per energy range--

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.210
is distinctly different.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:13.260
And the smaller the size
or the smaller dimension

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:16.020
that we're dealing with,
the more the system

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:24.050
becomes like a molecule
with these discrete states.

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:28.040
Now, when we are in the
regime of the discrete states,

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:30.200
we have new phenomenon.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:32.210
And that's the kind
of thing that I'm

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:34.880
going to emphasize
in my discussion

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:37.070
of the bismuth
nanowires where we're

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:41.030
interested in going to
smaller and smaller sizes

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:45.380
where the system shows quantum
effects, that phenomenon that

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:49.100
are not present in the bulk.

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:53.600
Now I'd like to tell you how I
got into the bismuth nanowire

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:55.260
business.

00:06:55.260 --> 00:07:01.170
And this was motivated by
practical considerations.

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:04.340
One of the nice things about
being in an engineering

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:10.580
department is we have a push
and pull between the practical

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:16.340
and the science-based studies.

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:19.400
So my initial
motivation for this

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:23.870
came from a practical
driver, and this

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:29.130
is interest in making
thermoelectrics that are better

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:32.720
than present thermoelectrics.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:36.240
Thermoelectrics have
two applications.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:39.590
One is for cooling
purposes, and the other

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:44.000
is for energy conversion
at high temperature.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:51.740
And here is a picture diagram
of the thermoelectric figure

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:53.270
of merit.

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:57.860
For our present discussion, this
isn't particularly important

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:02.750
except to say that the
state-of-the-art has a value

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:05.570
of this quantity,
figure of merit, ZT,

00:08:05.570 --> 00:08:08.330
dimensionless quantity, of 1.

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:14.900
And it's been at this value
since approximately 1960.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:19.370
If thermoelectrics could have
their figure of merit increased

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:23.330
to a factor of 3, they could be
competitive to a refrigerator,

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:24.860
something that we know.

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:27.860
And it would change the
economics and interest

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:32.460
in this kind of applications
area enormously.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:34.820
However, nobody
has figured out how

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:42.530
to do this, and not for lack
of effort but lack of ideas.

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:47.450
We had an idea about how to
change this a few years ago

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:50.240
by going to low dimensions.

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:56.150
And you could see this picture
is sort of a visionary picture.

00:08:56.150 --> 00:09:00.540
And it said if you could make
a one-dimensional bismuth,

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:03.150
or two-dimensional,
for that matter,

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:09.600
we could exceed the ZT of 1
with small sizes of either

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:13.500
a quantum well of bismuth
or a quantum wire of bismuth

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:17.820
and in fact get values that are
phenomenally bigger than even

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:20.760
what you could do
with a refrigerator.

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:25.170
However, there's a long way
to go from the actuality

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:29.760
and this phantom dream.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:33.750
The material, the actual
material that might do this

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:36.850
would be this bismuth
that I mentioned,

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:40.110
which has some
astounding properties.

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:44.250
Of all materials, it has a
constant energy surface--

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:46.500
we call this a Fermi surface--

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:50.040
that is more anisotropic
than any other material.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:54.750
Anisotropy in the Fermi
surface is very, very desirable

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:59.580
for thermoelectricity, as is
multiple carrier pockets, which

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:02.460
is another feature of bismuth.

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:05.740
Bismuth also has a
very high mobility,

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:09.900
which is another promising
feature for thermoelectrics.

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:13.020
And because it is
a very heavy atom,

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:16.080
it has a very low
thermal conductivity.

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:17.880
And for all of
these reasons, this

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:23.030
should be the ideal material
to be used for thermoelectric.

00:10:23.030 --> 00:10:26.300
However, the
problem with bismuth

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:28.280
and the reason why
bismuth has never

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:31.640
succeeded as a
thermoelectric has

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:36.770
to do with the following
point, property.

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:41.810
Bismuth is a semimetal with
an equal number of electrons

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:51.080
and holes so that the top of
the valance span, shown here,

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:54.890
is higher in energy than the
bottom of the conduction band.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:59.090
So it's sort of a semiconductor
with a negative bandgap.

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:03.170
So you always have both
electrons and holes present.

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:08.720
And for thermoelectricity, the
contribution of the electrons

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:11.600
essentially cancels
that of the holes,

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:15.560
and it is a very poor
thermoelectric material

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:17.280
for that reason.

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:21.800
However, if you could figure out
some way to make this material

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:25.540
either totally n-type
or totally p-type--

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:29.030
and for thermoelectric, you
have to be able to do both--

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:32.790
then this could be
very, very interesting.

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:35.360
So here is the idea.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:40.850
And this is an idea that we
had simultaneously almost

00:11:40.850 --> 00:11:45.230
with the idea of
doing low-dimensional

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:47.120
thermoelectrics.

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:49.550
What I have shown
on this picture

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:54.680
here is the subband structure
or the first subband

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:59.900
for electrons related to
the band edge for the bulk

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:03.230
and the first subband
which is below the bandgap

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:08.220
edge for the holes, as shown
on your left-hand side.

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:12.710
So now, as the size of the
quantum well or the quantum

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:17.510
wire decreases, the
difference in energy

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:22.980
between the first subband
and the band edge increases.

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:26.420
So this subband
edge will move up.

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:30.320
And for the holes, this
subband edge will move down.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:34.700
And eventually at some value
of the size of your quantum

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:39.920
well or your quantum wire, a
semiconductor will be formed.

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:42.470
And once you have
a semiconductor,

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:46.100
you could make that
either n or p-type.

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:48.700
So that was the
initial motivation

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:50.930
of us studying bismuth.

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:53.030
And we talked somebody
into giving us

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:54.800
some money to do this.

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:59.960
So we started work
on this project.

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:03.200
But an idea was
necessary because it

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:07.580
isn't easy to make any
low-dimensional structures

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:09.920
with bismuth because
bismuth diffuses

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:12.170
into almost everything.

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:15.620
So we had to devise
some mechanism

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:20.430
to have quantum-confined system.

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:24.950
And the idea came through
a public service activity

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:28.310
for Jackie Ying, who's a
collaborator on this project.

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:32.540
And she invited me to
tell chemical engineers

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:34.790
about carbon nanotubes.

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:37.190
And while they were
listening to me tell them

00:13:37.190 --> 00:13:38.930
about carbon
nanotubes, I thought

00:13:38.930 --> 00:13:42.690
I should listen to them
tell me about catalysis.

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:46.070
So I listened to a
lecture and heard

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:52.880
about molecular sieves and my
cells that will self-orient.

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:56.900
This is used in the
oil recovery business.

00:13:56.900 --> 00:13:58.460
They have, of
course, no interest

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:01.830
in making low-dimensional
semiconductors.

00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:04.820
But in listening to
them, I had the idea

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:09.020
what they do is make
self-organized holes

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:10.730
in a template.

00:14:10.730 --> 00:14:14.310
So they make something
that looks like this.

00:14:14.310 --> 00:14:16.490
And when I saw this,
I said, viola, this

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:21.350
is exactly what we need for
low-dimensional thermoelectrics

00:14:21.350 --> 00:14:24.290
and many other
scientific applications.

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:29.870
So we start out here
with an aluminum film.

00:14:29.870 --> 00:14:31.790
And so that's like
aluminum foil.

00:14:31.790 --> 00:14:33.920
It's a little bit
maybe higher purity

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:35.900
and we polish the
surface, et cetera,

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:38.600
anodize this electrochemically.

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:41.390
And there's a
competing mechanism

00:14:41.390 --> 00:14:43.370
that forms these holes.

00:14:43.370 --> 00:14:45.830
And depending on
the acid that's used

00:14:45.830 --> 00:14:48.870
for the electrochemical
treatment, the length of time,

00:14:48.870 --> 00:14:50.930
the voltage, current
conditions, you

00:14:50.930 --> 00:14:55.470
can control the size of
the pores, et cetera.

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:57.680
So these pores are made.

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:01.220
And then we use another
acid to take off

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:06.620
the bottom and high pressure
to fill the channels that are

00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:09.380
formed eventually with fluids--

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:14.160
in this case, bismuth, but you
can put in magnetic fluids,

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:17.780
something goes superconducting,
many, many possibilities.

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:20.630
And for some applications,
we would like

00:15:20.630 --> 00:15:23.120
to measure individual wires.

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:25.430
So we dissolve this template.

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:29.990
We have individual wires to
make for probe measurements

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:31.730
on individual wires.

00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:35.990
That's where Hank
Smith's help has come in.

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:39.950
So Jackie Ying taught us
how to make these templates

00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:43.730
and helped us with the
filling of the templates.

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:50.655
And we had a lot of help
from Professor Smith

00:15:50.655 --> 00:15:53.060
with his kind introduction.

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:54.300
But he's done more than that.

00:15:54.300 --> 00:16:00.470
He's helped us to make
transport measurements.

00:16:00.470 --> 00:16:03.620
Our earliest work was done.

00:16:03.620 --> 00:16:06.140
This is our first attempt.

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:08.410
And you see here, first student.

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:10.790
So these wires are a little
bit on the large side,

00:16:10.790 --> 00:16:14.300
65 nanometers, just
at the junction

00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:17.270
of being called nanostructures.

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:23.290
And so looking down top-on,
you could see these cylinders.

00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:29.510
They're not so perfect, but
the length-to-diameter ratio is

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:30.630
about 1,000--

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:31.355
1,000 aspect.

00:16:31.355 --> 00:16:33.920
So they're long, skinny things.

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:38.570
And the density of wires
here was approximately 10

00:16:38.570 --> 00:16:41.540
to the 10th wires per
square centimeters.

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:44.420
We do more than that
with the smaller wires.

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:47.600
So there were a lot of
wires that are made.

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:55.160
And I'd like to show Yumeng's
more recent work with improved

00:16:55.160 --> 00:16:56.820
processing step.

00:16:56.820 --> 00:16:59.630
And this is a much
smaller diameter wire.

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:02.390
And they're more perfect
than the large ones

00:17:02.390 --> 00:17:04.349
that the previous student made.

00:17:04.349 --> 00:17:06.530
And now we're down
to 18 nanometers,

00:17:06.530 --> 00:17:07.910
and these aren't so bad.

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:10.440
We have ideas for
improving them further.

00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:14.810
So the technology is
challenging to us,

00:17:14.810 --> 00:17:17.930
but our electrical
engineering students

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:22.280
seem to be able to handle this
and make progress with it.

00:17:22.280 --> 00:17:26.190
When we take the templates,
dissolve the templates,

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:29.370
you see here the
freestanding wires.

00:17:29.370 --> 00:17:31.860
They're very long and skinny.

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:34.680
And with
high-resolution TEM, you

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:37.230
could see that they're
fairly straight.

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:41.490
And these striations show grain
boundary structures, not very

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:44.460
many grain boundaries but
some grain boundaries.

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:46.860
And the size of these
grain boundaries

00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:51.690
is about the diameter
of the tubes.

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:58.950
So this is something that
we're very interested in.

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:04.520
Now, the nanowires would
be of relatively little use

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:08.700
were it not for this great
property that they have,

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:15.660
that the nanowires are single
crystals with common axis

00:18:15.660 --> 00:18:17.280
along the wires.

00:18:17.280 --> 00:18:21.810
So a given wire will
have a common axis,

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:27.000
but all the wires in the array,
the 10 to the 10th or so wires,

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:32.040
will have the same common
axis, most of them,

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:34.350
a high percentage.

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:38.670
And you could see this as
the X-ray diffraction pattern

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:42.820
with very sharp lines so it
looks like a single crystal.

00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:45.780
Now, what we found
recently-- in our first work,

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:48.720
we were making
bigger sized wires.

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:54.840
And all the wires had
the orientation 101.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:57.180
And we thought that they'd
all came out that way.

00:18:57.180 --> 00:18:59.380
But as the wire
diameters got smaller,

00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:02.670
we have a new preferred
orientation that

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:05.400
goes from 40 down to about 20.

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:07.800
And we haven't really
done much characterization

00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:12.060
below that length, but we
suspect that they're also

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:13.560
single crystals.

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:16.530
So this is an
important property.

00:19:16.530 --> 00:19:18.630
Why is it important?

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:21.480
All the wires are the same so
they have a common property.

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:23.670
So we can characterize them.

00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:28.465
And because of this, we're
able to have very long mean

00:19:28.465 --> 00:19:30.150
free paths.

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:33.450
We have a property that's called
ballistic transport that I'll

00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:38.250
describe in a little bit that
says that the electrons will

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:41.640
move for very long distances
before being scattered,

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:45.550
for distances that are much
longer than the wire diameters.

00:19:45.550 --> 00:19:48.640
So that's an important
consideration.

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:51.510
Another thing that I should
mention about this that makes

00:19:51.510 --> 00:19:57.330
this property quite remarkable
is that the crystal structure--

00:19:57.330 --> 00:19:59.730
that is, these
diffraction peaks--

00:19:59.730 --> 00:20:03.720
are exactly at the same
places, same angles as they

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:05.610
are in bulk bismuth.

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:09.330
So we have basically bulk
bismuth that's put in the wire.

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:12.900
So almost everything that we
know about bulk bismuth also

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:14.670
applies to the nanowires.

00:20:14.670 --> 00:20:21.720
This was an unexpected, lovely
property for these nanowires.

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:24.450
Now, other people have tried
to make nanowires before

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:28.480
of this sort, but this was
the first successful attempt

00:20:28.480 --> 00:20:31.490
of making single-crystal
nanowires, certainly

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:33.270
of bismuth.

00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:37.450
Now, how do we know that
they're all the same?

00:20:37.450 --> 00:20:40.920
Well, we have an X-ray
diffraction pattern

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:42.690
which gives you the
diffraction pattern

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:44.820
of many, many nanowires.

00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:47.700
And then we have an
electron diffraction pattern

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:50.490
for single, selected nanowires.

00:20:50.490 --> 00:20:53.400
And so from this
pattern, we can tell what

00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:56.110
the orientation happens to be.

00:20:56.110 --> 00:21:00.250
And that orientation is the same
as for the X-ray diffraction.

00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:05.220
So we know that not only
do we have single crystals,

00:21:05.220 --> 00:21:09.810
but they're all the same,
close to all the same.

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:12.010
Obviously there's
some deviation.

00:21:12.010 --> 00:21:16.680
You never have perfection
in a project like this.

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:19.080
The Raman spectra
is also interesting.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:23.640
And I show, on top, the Raman
spectra for bulk bismuth.

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:25.620
And because it's
such a heavy atom,

00:21:25.620 --> 00:21:29.410
the vibrational modes are
at very low frequency.

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:31.590
And when we make the
nanowires, you'll

00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:36.130
see that the same
frequencies appear.

00:21:36.130 --> 00:21:38.250
So we have the same
mode structure,

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:40.440
but the relative
intensities are different.

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:42.180
And this is an area
that we hope to be

00:21:42.180 --> 00:21:45.370
studying in the near future.

00:21:45.370 --> 00:21:48.300
So to summarize about
the remarkable structure

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:51.470
of the nanowires--

00:21:51.470 --> 00:21:56.970
so here's the
structural properties.

00:21:56.970 --> 00:22:03.090
We have narrow distribution of
wire diameters, less than 10%.

00:22:03.090 --> 00:22:06.180
They're quite uniform
along the length-- again,

00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:08.250
much less than 10%.

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:12.210
They're single crystals with
a common axis along the wire

00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:17.220
but otherwise randomly oriented
in the cross-sectional area

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:21.900
with respect to each other,
not with respect to each one

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:23.850
but with respect to each other.

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:27.090
And they have the same crystal
structure as bulk bismuth.

00:22:30.966 --> 00:22:35.010
The template material in which
the bismuth nanowires sit

00:22:35.010 --> 00:22:39.490
is a wide-gap semiconductor,
3 and 1/2 electron volts.

00:22:39.490 --> 00:22:42.740
So we have quantum-confined
electrons in the wires.

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:45.990
They don't talk to each other.

00:22:45.990 --> 00:22:49.040
And thermal conductivity
is very low,

00:22:49.040 --> 00:22:52.770
and a bunch of other
interesting properties--

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:57.360
dielectric constants very
high, a few other interesting

00:22:57.360 --> 00:22:58.680
properties.

00:22:58.680 --> 00:23:03.150
Now I'm going to talk about
the unusual properties.

00:23:03.150 --> 00:23:05.130
Up until now, we talked
about the structure.

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:07.930
Now I'm going to talk
about the properties.

00:23:07.930 --> 00:23:12.870
So the first thing was the
semimetal-semiconductor

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:14.430
transition.

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:18.060
And just to remind
you what that is--

00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:23.100
I hope I can find my little
viewgraph that showed that.

00:23:23.100 --> 00:23:25.330
Here we go.

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:31.370
So this is the basic viewgraph.

00:23:31.370 --> 00:23:33.880
What we're looking
at, we're looking

00:23:33.880 --> 00:23:39.040
for that diameter
for the wire where

00:23:39.040 --> 00:23:44.860
this subband for the
holes crosses the highest

00:23:44.860 --> 00:23:47.470
electron subband
for the electrons

00:23:47.470 --> 00:23:49.330
and a band gap is formed.

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:52.330
So that's the concept of
what we're looking for.

00:23:52.330 --> 00:23:59.710
And now I'd like to give a few
examples of its observation.

00:23:59.710 --> 00:24:03.520
So we have optical properties,
transport properties,

00:24:03.520 --> 00:24:08.230
and several other properties.

00:24:08.230 --> 00:24:12.900
So now to get a little
bit more specific

00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:20.660
about the bismuth structure,
I have to show you--

00:24:20.660 --> 00:24:23.300
bismuth is a
complicated material.

00:24:23.300 --> 00:24:25.490
And if it weren't
complicated, most

00:24:25.490 --> 00:24:29.840
of the things I'm talking
about would not happen.

00:24:29.840 --> 00:24:33.200
So we have the L-point electron.

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:36.650
So these are the
L-point electrons.

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:39.770
And there are multiple
carrier pockets

00:24:39.770 --> 00:24:45.260
which will have the same
energy in the bulk material.

00:24:45.260 --> 00:24:48.380
But once we make
nanowires out of them,

00:24:48.380 --> 00:24:52.100
then we have a splitting
in energy between them,

00:24:52.100 --> 00:24:57.290
and that complicates our
thinking about the problem.

00:24:57.290 --> 00:25:00.590
But for this audience
and the simple picture,

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:04.640
we'll neglect that
for the most part.

00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:06.860
And here are the holes.

00:25:06.860 --> 00:25:12.170
And if we didn't have the
rhombohedral crystal symmetry

00:25:12.170 --> 00:25:16.355
and this were cubic, the
T-point holes and the T

00:25:16.355 --> 00:25:18.380
and L point would
be all the same,

00:25:18.380 --> 00:25:22.070
and we wouldn't have a semimetal
so that the special properties

00:25:22.070 --> 00:25:26.120
of bismuth that allow us to
make a semiconductor out of it

00:25:26.120 --> 00:25:29.900
by going to low
dimensions are bound up,

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:32.660
are closely connected
with the fact

00:25:32.660 --> 00:25:37.040
that bismuth is anisotropic.

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:41.290
OK, so this is an
important consideration.

00:25:41.290 --> 00:25:44.680
I'll return to
this at some point.

00:25:44.680 --> 00:25:50.080
And now, just, I'd
like to mention

00:25:50.080 --> 00:25:54.490
about the
semimetal-semiconductor

00:25:54.490 --> 00:25:56.080
transition.

00:25:56.080 --> 00:25:59.930
So here's our picture for
the semimetal-semiconductor

00:25:59.930 --> 00:26:01.740
transition.

00:26:01.740 --> 00:26:05.940
At large values of
the wire diameter,

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:11.050
then the separation that
we're talking about here

00:26:11.050 --> 00:26:14.470
would be a band
overlap of bismuth,

00:26:14.470 --> 00:26:18.760
which is about 40 millivolts.

00:26:18.760 --> 00:26:21.850
What's shown here, for the
direction that the wires

00:26:21.850 --> 00:26:29.650
actually grow, we have
the A-point pockets

00:26:29.650 --> 00:26:33.640
that are along this
cross-section, which

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:39.370
is the mirror plane of the
reciprocal lattice zone,

00:26:39.370 --> 00:26:43.480
this carrier pocket is
different from the B and C.

00:26:43.480 --> 00:26:45.460
The details of that
are not terribly

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:48.190
important for our
present discussion.

00:26:48.190 --> 00:26:52.250
In the limit that the
wires are very large,

00:26:52.250 --> 00:26:55.900
then these two lines
become equal to each other

00:26:55.900 --> 00:27:00.980
and we have a common value
for this band overlap.

00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:04.670
As the wire dimension gets
smaller, we get a splitting.

00:27:04.670 --> 00:27:09.620
And this shows the splitting
that makes the A and B and C

00:27:09.620 --> 00:27:10.740
different.

00:27:10.740 --> 00:27:13.640
And the reason
for that has to do

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:17.210
with the relation of the
symmetry of these carrier

00:27:17.210 --> 00:27:20.150
pockets and the
growth direction which

00:27:20.150 --> 00:27:24.830
are not along a common
high-symmetry axis.

00:27:24.830 --> 00:27:30.420
The important point is that
as the diameter gets smaller,

00:27:30.420 --> 00:27:34.340
eventually we get a crossover
with this whole band level.

00:27:34.340 --> 00:27:39.530
This is the highest conduction
subband for the holes.

00:27:39.530 --> 00:27:44.870
And at this point, we cross
this A-point carrier pocket.

00:27:44.870 --> 00:27:48.620
And at this point, we cross
the B and C carrier pocket.

00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:51.560
And at this point right
here, 50 nanometers,

00:27:51.560 --> 00:27:55.250
we make the
semimetal-semiconductor

00:27:55.250 --> 00:27:56.300
transition.

00:27:56.300 --> 00:27:58.220
So that's what
we're talking about.

00:27:58.220 --> 00:28:01.010
And what is the
evidence that we have

00:28:01.010 --> 00:28:03.530
for observing this phenomenon?

00:28:03.530 --> 00:28:08.600
[INAUDIBLE] where's the
Fermi energy [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:08.600 --> 00:28:13.220
Well, the Fermi energy will
be somewhere between these two

00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:16.160
such that the
number of electrons

00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:19.097
is equal to the
number of holes, OK?

00:28:19.097 --> 00:28:20.505
[INAUDIBLE]

00:28:20.505 --> 00:28:21.005
Hm?

00:28:21.005 --> 00:28:22.440
[INAUDIBLE]

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:30.690
It goes right through here
if you have [INAUDIBLE]..

00:28:35.750 --> 00:28:37.520
So how would this be observed?

00:28:37.520 --> 00:28:40.130
Now, the most prosaic
and simple observation

00:28:40.130 --> 00:28:43.820
is to do an optical
absorption experiment.

00:28:43.820 --> 00:28:47.720
So if you have a
semiconductor, then you

00:28:47.720 --> 00:28:52.790
have a transparent situation,
very low absorption.

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:55.160
And if you have a
semimetal, you'll

00:28:55.160 --> 00:28:59.250
have relatively high absorption,
and that's what's shown here.

00:28:59.250 --> 00:29:02.960
So this is a very crude,
simple-minded picture

00:29:02.960 --> 00:29:04.670
of what you might see.

00:29:04.670 --> 00:29:07.070
And so this is the
first experiment we did.

00:29:07.070 --> 00:29:11.000
So that's like a
flashlight-type experiment.

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:14.630
And we're trying now to do
this much more quantitatively.

00:29:14.630 --> 00:29:18.560
And that's the thesis
of Marcie Black.

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:20.240
And she's making good
progress with it.

00:29:20.240 --> 00:29:24.620
So hopefully, soon
we'll hear more details.

00:29:24.620 --> 00:29:28.860
The transport properties
are quite interesting.

00:29:28.860 --> 00:29:31.100
So here is bulk bismuth.

00:29:31.100 --> 00:29:34.744
So this is resistance normalized
to the room temperature value.

00:29:34.744 --> 00:29:36.160
And I'll explain
in a little while

00:29:36.160 --> 00:29:38.340
why we have to do it that way.

00:29:38.340 --> 00:29:39.560
So this is bulk bismuth.

00:29:39.560 --> 00:29:41.960
And you see when we
get to the nanowires,

00:29:41.960 --> 00:29:43.880
the temperature dependence
of the resistance

00:29:43.880 --> 00:29:46.850
is very, very different
from bulk bismuth.

00:29:46.850 --> 00:29:49.540
And in fact, these
wires down here

00:29:49.540 --> 00:29:55.510
are the semimetallic wires while
those wires up there are all

00:29:55.510 --> 00:29:57.530
semiconducting.

00:29:57.530 --> 00:29:59.840
And you could see that
the behavior is very, very

00:29:59.840 --> 00:30:00.990
different.

00:30:00.990 --> 00:30:04.190
And what determines
temperature-dependent

00:30:04.190 --> 00:30:08.300
resistance is
always an interplay

00:30:08.300 --> 00:30:10.100
between the
temperature dependence

00:30:10.100 --> 00:30:13.340
of the carrier density,
which for a semiconductor

00:30:13.340 --> 00:30:15.200
is very strong.

00:30:15.200 --> 00:30:16.730
And the second
effect, of course,

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:20.360
is the temperature dependence
of a mobility, which

00:30:20.360 --> 00:30:24.050
is more important for
the semimetallic phase

00:30:24.050 --> 00:30:28.220
so that the interplay between
these and the fact also

00:30:28.220 --> 00:30:32.960
that the semi-conducting,
semimetal properties are also

00:30:32.960 --> 00:30:36.810
temperature-dependent
is what's responsible

00:30:36.810 --> 00:30:38.890
for this complex behavior.

00:30:38.890 --> 00:30:43.410
So these are the experiments
done for a number of nanowires.

00:30:43.410 --> 00:30:46.200
And this is Yumeng's
recent calculation

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:48.270
for his master's thesis.

00:30:48.270 --> 00:30:51.360
And here's 70-- no
adjustable parameters here.

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:55.890
Here is 70 nanometers,
and here is 70 nanometers

00:30:55.890 --> 00:30:56.860
for the experiment.

00:30:56.860 --> 00:30:59.200
That looks pretty good.

00:30:59.200 --> 00:31:03.670
And the 36 is shown there.

00:31:03.670 --> 00:31:09.060
So this much we are
able now to explain

00:31:09.060 --> 00:31:11.970
with a simple calculation.

00:31:11.970 --> 00:31:16.060
And I'll get to that in a
moment, what we do about that.

00:31:16.060 --> 00:31:18.510
But there are still
details to be filled in.

00:31:18.510 --> 00:31:20.550
We don't have all
the answers yet,

00:31:20.550 --> 00:31:24.870
but this is what we've
accomplished thus far.

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:27.570
At low temperatures,
very low temperatures

00:31:27.570 --> 00:31:31.720
where localization effects are
beginning to become important--

00:31:31.720 --> 00:31:34.240
below 10 degrees,
localization effects

00:31:34.240 --> 00:31:37.270
become important, like
in the 2 degree range.

00:31:37.270 --> 00:31:43.330
That you see here more clearly
that for the semiconducting

00:31:43.330 --> 00:31:49.450
tubes, the coefficient of
resistance with temperature

00:31:49.450 --> 00:31:53.350
is positive for the
semimetal phase going up.

00:31:53.350 --> 00:31:55.930
And for the conducting
phase, it's going down.

00:31:55.930 --> 00:31:59.830
And there are no
exceptions for that.

00:31:59.830 --> 00:32:02.990
The situation for
the magnetoresistance

00:32:02.990 --> 00:32:07.790
is quite interesting
also but more complicated

00:32:07.790 --> 00:32:10.770
for an explanation, so I
didn't bring that along.

00:32:10.770 --> 00:32:14.870
But it fits in with the general
picture that I've told you.

00:32:14.870 --> 00:32:19.490
This viewgraph shows the
more complicated situation

00:32:19.490 --> 00:32:24.440
which underlines the temperature
dependence of the properties

00:32:24.440 --> 00:32:26.660
of the bismuth nanowires.

00:32:26.660 --> 00:32:30.230
So if we have a
very small nanowire,

00:32:30.230 --> 00:32:34.391
like a 10-nanometer diameter
nanowire, then all the way

00:32:34.391 --> 00:32:36.890
from the lowest temperature all
the way to room temperature,

00:32:36.890 --> 00:32:38.840
it behaves like a semiconductor.

00:32:38.840 --> 00:32:41.540
And then you see, for
many orders of magnitude,

00:32:41.540 --> 00:32:43.530
the carrier density
is going to change.

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:45.620
This is a plot of
carrier density

00:32:45.620 --> 00:32:49.580
with an exponential scale as
a function of temperature.

00:32:49.580 --> 00:32:54.320
On the other hand, if we
have an 80-nanometer wire,

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:58.060
then it's always semimetallic
independent of temperature

00:32:58.060 --> 00:32:59.730
for this entire range.

00:32:59.730 --> 00:33:03.650
And you see that the dependence
of that carrier density

00:33:03.650 --> 00:33:06.410
in that case is very minor.

00:33:06.410 --> 00:33:10.460
So this shows you about the
interplay between the carrier

00:33:10.460 --> 00:33:14.330
density for the different kinds
of nanowires, temperature,

00:33:14.330 --> 00:33:18.200
and resistivity
transport properties.

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:21.770
Now, for an intermediate
case, 40 nanometers

00:33:21.770 --> 00:33:24.530
at low temperatures behaves
like a semiconductor,

00:33:24.530 --> 00:33:27.030
at high temperature
behaves like a semimetal.

00:33:27.030 --> 00:33:30.410
So this wire undergoes a
semiconductor-semimetal

00:33:30.410 --> 00:33:33.200
transition as a
function of temperature.

00:33:33.200 --> 00:33:35.480
And that is part
of the explanation

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:37.760
that we showed you for
the temperature-dependent

00:33:37.760 --> 00:33:40.750
transport.

00:33:40.750 --> 00:33:43.720
Now, the next phenomenon
that I'd like to talk about

00:33:43.720 --> 00:33:49.140
is ballistic transport
in these materials.

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:52.300
What is ballistic transport?

00:33:52.300 --> 00:33:56.680
This phenomenon occurs when
the mean free path is large

00:33:56.680 --> 00:34:00.950
compared to the
size of the sample.

00:34:00.950 --> 00:34:06.970
And when that happens, you can
have wave propagation instead

00:34:06.970 --> 00:34:11.270
of particle propagation.

00:34:11.270 --> 00:34:16.420
Now, in our case, we
use a magnetic field

00:34:16.420 --> 00:34:19.090
to demonstrate this phenomenon.

00:34:19.090 --> 00:34:22.179
And I'll show you
how we do that.

00:34:24.739 --> 00:34:35.360
So the next viewgraph shows how
we do transport measurements

00:34:35.360 --> 00:34:37.940
on our nanowire arrays.

00:34:37.940 --> 00:34:42.230
And after, I'll show you how
we do it on a single nanowire.

00:34:42.230 --> 00:34:44.659
So here we have
many, many nanowires.

00:34:44.659 --> 00:34:46.540
We have 10 to the
10th nanowires.

00:34:46.540 --> 00:34:48.770
They were all lined up,
and we put electrode

00:34:48.770 --> 00:34:50.330
on the top and the bottom.

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:53.420
And we could measure
either resistance,

00:34:53.420 --> 00:34:55.280
or we could measure
magnetoresistance,

00:34:55.280 --> 00:34:57.830
resistance in the presence
of a magnetic field.

00:34:57.830 --> 00:35:00.260
We do both of those
kinds of experiments.

00:35:00.260 --> 00:35:03.140
And they give us a
lot of information.

00:35:03.140 --> 00:35:06.890
The information we cannot
get from this is what is

00:35:06.890 --> 00:35:10.190
the absolute value of the
resistance as a function

00:35:10.190 --> 00:35:11.040
of temperature.

00:35:11.040 --> 00:35:13.310
And the reason for
that is we don't

00:35:13.310 --> 00:35:18.080
know how many nanowires we have
in our sample that are actually

00:35:18.080 --> 00:35:20.540
contacting the electrodes.

00:35:20.540 --> 00:35:24.660
And we have no way of measuring
that at the present time.

00:35:24.660 --> 00:35:27.890
So we have to do normalized
measurements when

00:35:27.890 --> 00:35:30.090
we're working in this format.

00:35:30.090 --> 00:35:34.190
The other problem, is that we
have some contact resistance

00:35:34.190 --> 00:35:36.530
so that two-point
measurements never

00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:40.590
give you very accurate
absolute measurements.

00:35:40.590 --> 00:35:42.440
However, these kinds
of measurements

00:35:42.440 --> 00:35:47.870
are fine for looking
for phenomenon

00:35:47.870 --> 00:35:50.360
like ballistic transport.

00:35:50.360 --> 00:35:56.620
So this viewgraph demonstrates
ballistic transport

00:35:56.620 --> 00:35:59.180
in some way.

00:35:59.180 --> 00:36:04.430
So for example, the black
curve is taken at 100 Kelvin.

00:36:04.430 --> 00:36:07.100
And you just see an
increasing function

00:36:07.100 --> 00:36:10.670
of the resistance as a
function of magnetic field.

00:36:10.670 --> 00:36:15.190
However, when we get down
to lower temperatures,

00:36:15.190 --> 00:36:17.420
you see that there's a peak.

00:36:17.420 --> 00:36:19.550
There's a maximum.

00:36:19.550 --> 00:36:24.680
The maximum has to do
with the following effect.

00:36:24.680 --> 00:36:30.290
At low magnetic
fields, the electrons

00:36:30.290 --> 00:36:32.760
are making large orbits.

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:37.310
So they tend to bump
into the edge of the wire

00:36:37.310 --> 00:36:40.220
more frequently than
they would normally

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:43.880
because the magnetic field
deflects magnetic fields

00:36:43.880 --> 00:36:46.640
along the wire
direction, magnetic field

00:36:46.640 --> 00:36:49.710
deflects the path
of the electrons

00:36:49.710 --> 00:36:54.410
so they intersect with
the edge more frequently.

00:36:54.410 --> 00:36:56.540
And therefore, they
have more scattering.

00:36:56.540 --> 00:36:59.480
And therefore, the
resistance goes up.

00:36:59.480 --> 00:37:03.290
However, when the magnetic
field gets large enough

00:37:03.290 --> 00:37:06.200
to start containing
the electrons to move

00:37:06.200 --> 00:37:08.810
within the wire
diameter, then we

00:37:08.810 --> 00:37:11.600
have less scattering
than we had before.

00:37:11.600 --> 00:37:15.590
And therefore, the
resistance goes down.

00:37:15.590 --> 00:37:17.330
Well, that's a hypothesis.

00:37:17.330 --> 00:37:19.880
And now I'll show you all
the different parameters

00:37:19.880 --> 00:37:24.110
that we try is in agreement
with that hypothesis.

00:37:24.110 --> 00:37:25.700
I'll show you a few
of them, but we've

00:37:25.700 --> 00:37:28.400
done quite a number
of others besides what

00:37:28.400 --> 00:37:30.140
I'm showing you here.

00:37:30.140 --> 00:37:33.900
Here is the comparison
for two diameters.

00:37:33.900 --> 00:37:36.260
So if you have a
smaller diameter,

00:37:36.260 --> 00:37:43.640
it takes a larger magnetic field
to have the orbit contained

00:37:43.640 --> 00:37:45.500
within the wire diameter.

00:37:45.500 --> 00:37:49.130
So for the smaller
diameters, the critical field

00:37:49.130 --> 00:37:52.610
is higher than for the larger
diameters, as is shown here.

00:37:52.610 --> 00:37:55.310
And the dependence
actually goes like, 1,

00:37:55.310 --> 00:37:59.140
over the diameter in
the inverse diameter.

00:37:59.140 --> 00:38:01.220
And we have a nice plot of that.

00:38:01.220 --> 00:38:05.660
So the picture of what's going
on is shown in this viewgraph.

00:38:05.660 --> 00:38:12.230
So for zero field, of course
you have some scattering.

00:38:12.230 --> 00:38:16.850
But as the field
increases a little bit,

00:38:16.850 --> 00:38:22.790
the orbits are deflected so
that there's increased contact

00:38:22.790 --> 00:38:23.570
with the wire.

00:38:23.570 --> 00:38:25.140
So the resistance goes up.

00:38:25.140 --> 00:38:28.430
But then when the high
magnetic fields are reached

00:38:28.430 --> 00:38:30.380
and we get these
circular orbits,

00:38:30.380 --> 00:38:34.640
then [? we tend to ?] start
missing the interaction

00:38:34.640 --> 00:38:35.940
with the boundaries.

00:38:35.940 --> 00:38:39.230
And we can see this
ballistic transport effect.

00:38:39.230 --> 00:38:43.250
So now, if the magnetic field
instead of being along the wire

00:38:43.250 --> 00:38:46.310
is transverse to the
wire, we should not

00:38:46.310 --> 00:38:48.500
see this effect at all.

00:38:48.500 --> 00:38:52.580
So OK, here we are at transverse
magnetic field independent

00:38:52.580 --> 00:38:53.800
of temperature.

00:38:53.800 --> 00:38:58.100
They're all
approximately quadratic.

00:38:58.100 --> 00:39:01.190
And with the
longitudinal field if you

00:39:01.190 --> 00:39:03.830
add some dopants
so that you make

00:39:03.830 --> 00:39:05.635
the mean free path
of the electrons--

00:39:05.635 --> 00:39:08.090
the electrons never
reach the wire boundary.

00:39:08.090 --> 00:39:10.310
They don't know the
wire boundary is there.

00:39:10.310 --> 00:39:13.538
So then it behaves
again quadratically.

00:39:17.030 --> 00:39:22.370
Another very interesting
phenomenon that occurs

00:39:22.370 --> 00:39:27.320
has to do with orbits
in a magnetic field

00:39:27.320 --> 00:39:29.990
at low temperatures.

00:39:29.990 --> 00:39:34.640
In our solid state course, we
talk about the magnetic length

00:39:34.640 --> 00:39:36.600
of electrons.

00:39:36.600 --> 00:39:42.230
So electrons in a magnetic field
experience the quantization

00:39:42.230 --> 00:39:44.810
of the magnetic energy levels.

00:39:44.810 --> 00:39:49.130
And the magnetic length is
given here by this relation.

00:39:49.130 --> 00:39:54.120
And this is a measure of
the size of an electron wave

00:39:54.120 --> 00:39:58.470
function in a magnetic field.

00:39:58.470 --> 00:40:03.960
When the wire diameter
and the magnetic length

00:40:03.960 --> 00:40:06.960
become approximately
equal to each other

00:40:06.960 --> 00:40:11.820
so that the magnetic
lengths are shown here

00:40:11.820 --> 00:40:18.180
for the various wires, as shown
on this picture, magnetic field

00:40:18.180 --> 00:40:21.660
corresponding to these
lengths, and when

00:40:21.660 --> 00:40:27.180
that length is equal to the wire
diameter, we have these lines.

00:40:27.180 --> 00:40:32.160
And you will see on these
curves that there are kinks.

00:40:32.160 --> 00:40:35.420
On the lowest one on
the top, 70 nanometers,

00:40:35.420 --> 00:40:38.000
you see quite a large kink.

00:40:38.000 --> 00:40:44.760
And for fields lower than
that point where that--

00:40:44.760 --> 00:40:47.190
well, I should say
that the kink occurs

00:40:47.190 --> 00:40:50.280
when one flux unit is
contained within the wire,

00:40:50.280 --> 00:40:54.060
one quantum flux
unit of magnetic flux

00:40:54.060 --> 00:40:58.260
is contained within the wire
diameter, there is an effect.

00:40:58.260 --> 00:41:03.600
Below that effect, we have
confinement because of the wire

00:41:03.600 --> 00:41:04.500
diameter.

00:41:04.500 --> 00:41:07.690
So that's a
one-dimensional effect.

00:41:07.690 --> 00:41:09.390
And for higher
magnetic fields, we

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:13.000
have confinement because
of the magnetic field.

00:41:13.000 --> 00:41:15.615
So the diameter, the size
of the wire, dimensionality

00:41:15.615 --> 00:41:17.980
of the wire, is not important.

00:41:17.980 --> 00:41:22.440
So we have a changeover
in effect in phenomenon

00:41:22.440 --> 00:41:24.780
and therefore,
different dependencies

00:41:24.780 --> 00:41:27.667
of the resistance on
magnetic field temperature

00:41:27.667 --> 00:41:28.500
and everything else.

00:41:31.830 --> 00:41:35.820
One of the drawbacks of
our measurement technique

00:41:35.820 --> 00:41:41.580
is that we don't know how many
wires we make contact with.

00:41:41.580 --> 00:41:46.530
So we have to do some kind of
measurement on single wires.

00:41:46.530 --> 00:41:50.670
And Hank has helped us
with his liftoff technique

00:41:50.670 --> 00:41:55.380
to allow us to make contacts
with individual wires.

00:41:55.380 --> 00:41:58.320
So here is an individual wire.

00:41:58.320 --> 00:42:01.170
And there were a lot of
processing steps that you see.

00:42:01.170 --> 00:42:06.810
We have a grid that's defined
photolithography, actually,

00:42:06.810 --> 00:42:08.150
by an electron beam.

00:42:08.150 --> 00:42:10.800
So we have a lot of points here.

00:42:10.800 --> 00:42:15.170
And we use these points to
locate where a given wire is.

00:42:15.170 --> 00:42:16.890
So here is a given wire.

00:42:16.890 --> 00:42:22.160
And the wire position relative
to some [INAUDIBLE] marks

00:42:22.160 --> 00:42:23.310
is noted.

00:42:23.310 --> 00:42:30.420
And then we apply
photolithographic techniques

00:42:30.420 --> 00:42:35.670
to attach leads to this wire.

00:42:35.670 --> 00:42:40.830
And we have a liftoff technique
that finally provides us

00:42:40.830 --> 00:42:44.520
with four contacts, two
of which are shown here.

00:42:44.520 --> 00:42:49.620
But we actually put four
contacts on the sample.

00:42:49.620 --> 00:42:54.970
And the resulting
picture is shown here.

00:42:54.970 --> 00:42:57.690
The four contacts are
the-- this cross gives you

00:42:57.690 --> 00:43:00.900
the four contacts and
their contact to big pads.

00:43:00.900 --> 00:43:04.280
And then we can do experiments
related to the big pads.

00:43:04.280 --> 00:43:09.240
But inside the middle of this
is the actual wire assembly.

00:43:09.240 --> 00:43:10.600
That's shown here.

00:43:10.600 --> 00:43:15.540
So here's our bismuth nanowire,
and here are the four contact

00:43:15.540 --> 00:43:16.390
points.

00:43:16.390 --> 00:43:19.880
So this allows us to do
voltage current measurements.

00:43:22.890 --> 00:43:25.960
We are at the earliest
possible stages with this,

00:43:25.960 --> 00:43:29.850
but we have our
first results showing

00:43:29.850 --> 00:43:33.190
that the absolute
resistivity here--

00:43:33.190 --> 00:43:35.880
so this current voltage
diagram, from the slope,

00:43:35.880 --> 00:43:37.770
you can get the resistivity.

00:43:37.770 --> 00:43:40.560
And the resistivity
is somewhat larger

00:43:40.560 --> 00:43:43.380
than that of bulk
bismuth, a factor of 6,

00:43:43.380 --> 00:43:47.880
showing a reduction in the
mobility of the carriers

00:43:47.880 --> 00:43:52.320
due to boundary scattering
and probably other defects.

00:43:52.320 --> 00:43:54.780
Now, we have a big
program ahead of us

00:43:54.780 --> 00:43:58.020
to measure this all as a
function of temperature

00:43:58.020 --> 00:44:00.420
and wire diameter.

00:44:00.420 --> 00:44:04.800
So this will keep us
busy for many months.

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:09.840
I would like now
to say something

00:44:09.840 --> 00:44:13.470
about how we go about
research in this field.

00:44:13.470 --> 00:44:20.190
And this is kind of almost
getting to concluding remarks.

00:44:20.190 --> 00:44:23.610
When we or any
other group starts

00:44:23.610 --> 00:44:27.900
working in low-dimensional
devices and physics,

00:44:27.900 --> 00:44:33.120
modeling becomes a very, very
important part of the exercise.

00:44:33.120 --> 00:44:35.490
Without modeling,
we never can make

00:44:35.490 --> 00:44:41.130
a device that will satisfy our
imagination for specifications

00:44:41.130 --> 00:44:43.770
because in something
like thermoelectricity--

00:44:43.770 --> 00:44:45.570
but it could be
almost anything else--

00:44:45.570 --> 00:44:49.380
it's very easy to
make your device worse

00:44:49.380 --> 00:44:51.930
than the
three-dimensional device.

00:44:51.930 --> 00:44:54.810
And only by very
careful selection

00:44:54.810 --> 00:44:57.810
of how to make the
design of the device will

00:44:57.810 --> 00:45:00.030
you actually be
better than you are

00:45:00.030 --> 00:45:02.470
in the three-dimensional system.

00:45:02.470 --> 00:45:05.490
So I'd like to
emphasize the importance

00:45:05.490 --> 00:45:09.570
of the concept of modeling
and then the use of modeling

00:45:09.570 --> 00:45:15.660
to actually determine the
design parameters to get

00:45:15.660 --> 00:45:17.260
a useful device.

00:45:17.260 --> 00:45:20.580
So this is kind of a new
way of doing device design.

00:45:20.580 --> 00:45:23.070
I know this is true in all
the other groups working

00:45:23.070 --> 00:45:24.820
with nanostructures.

00:45:24.820 --> 00:45:27.990
This is a different way
of looking at things.

00:45:27.990 --> 00:45:29.760
And I'll just make
a couple of comments

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:32.220
about the situation
in bismuth, and then

00:45:32.220 --> 00:45:36.750
I'll talk about some final
comments about doping

00:45:36.750 --> 00:45:40.830
the samples and that we
think we know how to do it,

00:45:40.830 --> 00:45:42.720
but it's a very
challenging thing

00:45:42.720 --> 00:45:45.480
to do that for
bismuth nanowires.

00:45:45.480 --> 00:45:49.030
When you start moving into
a new area and a new field

00:45:49.030 --> 00:45:52.650
and the great unknowns,
there are many issues

00:45:52.650 --> 00:45:57.060
that have not been previously
addressed by any investigator.

00:45:57.060 --> 00:46:01.110
So you have to be careful
that you get it right.

00:46:03.750 --> 00:46:07.530
Well the bismuth is
a complicated system

00:46:07.530 --> 00:46:08.909
because we have holes.

00:46:08.909 --> 00:46:09.825
Holes are pretty easy.

00:46:09.825 --> 00:46:15.790
It's just a diagonal [INAUDIBLE]
for common semiconductors.

00:46:15.790 --> 00:46:18.030
But the electrons are
much more complicated.

00:46:18.030 --> 00:46:21.720
The constant energy surfaces,
there are three of them.

00:46:21.720 --> 00:46:23.170
They're different
from each other,

00:46:23.170 --> 00:46:25.920
and they're very
elongated ellipsoids,

00:46:25.920 --> 00:46:27.990
not along high-symmetry
directions

00:46:27.990 --> 00:46:30.990
but they're tilted from the
high-symmetry directions.

00:46:30.990 --> 00:46:34.290
The other thing is that the
bandgap is so small that they

00:46:34.290 --> 00:46:36.450
couple closely to each other.

00:46:36.450 --> 00:46:39.450
So we cannot use the simple
parabolic model that we use

00:46:39.450 --> 00:46:43.170
for holes, but we have to have
coupled band models so that

00:46:43.170 --> 00:46:47.910
the calculation becomes
quite complex in a hurry.

00:46:47.910 --> 00:46:55.260
So now, we have some remarkably
good students at MIT.

00:46:55.260 --> 00:46:59.940
In concept, solving these
equations is straightforward.

00:46:59.940 --> 00:47:02.710
The implementation is
not straightforward.

00:47:02.710 --> 00:47:08.760
So Yumeng [? Lin ?] has just
finished his master's thesis

00:47:08.760 --> 00:47:12.000
and figured out a very
nice numerical solution

00:47:12.000 --> 00:47:15.390
for the Schrodinger
equation for the case

00:47:15.390 --> 00:47:20.010
of anisotropic surfaces,
multiple carrier pockets,

00:47:20.010 --> 00:47:22.590
and strongly coupled bands.

00:47:22.590 --> 00:47:24.810
And it's based on this
grid structure which

00:47:24.810 --> 00:47:27.660
allows us to do a
difference equation

00:47:27.660 --> 00:47:29.670
and a numerical solution.

00:47:29.670 --> 00:47:32.779
And the results have
been very interesting.

00:47:32.779 --> 00:47:34.320
And this is the
first time we've been

00:47:34.320 --> 00:47:38.460
able to get a quantitative
agreement with the experiments,

00:47:38.460 --> 00:47:40.200
as I showed you before.

00:47:43.350 --> 00:47:46.680
The next thing that has to
be done to make a device

00:47:46.680 --> 00:47:50.380
is to control the doping,
both n and p-type.

00:47:50.380 --> 00:47:53.130
The doping of n-type is
the one we started first

00:47:53.130 --> 00:47:56.280
because that one is easier
in the case of bismuth.

00:47:56.280 --> 00:47:58.365
The doping of p-type
is more complicated

00:47:58.365 --> 00:48:01.110
for reasons I might explain.

00:48:01.110 --> 00:48:06.540
And so first success
was obtained quickly.

00:48:06.540 --> 00:48:09.960
So on the top, you
see pure bismuth.

00:48:09.960 --> 00:48:12.510
And then the
connectivity increases

00:48:12.510 --> 00:48:14.780
by a couple of orders of
magnitude when you dope it.

00:48:14.780 --> 00:48:16.470
So you could see
that you could change

00:48:16.470 --> 00:48:19.530
the properties by doping.

00:48:19.530 --> 00:48:22.080
Bismuth is column five
of the periodic table.

00:48:22.080 --> 00:48:27.030
Doping with an element in column
six gives you electrons n-type.

00:48:27.030 --> 00:48:31.740
Doping with an element in column
four should give you p-type.

00:48:31.740 --> 00:48:34.710
And it sort of does
except for complications.

00:48:34.710 --> 00:48:39.200
So we can dope our system.

00:48:39.200 --> 00:48:41.780
And the next viewgraph
shows that we can change

00:48:41.780 --> 00:48:44.820
the electrical properties.

00:48:44.820 --> 00:48:48.650
So here's the
temperature dependence

00:48:48.650 --> 00:48:53.090
of the normalized
resistance for different--

00:48:53.090 --> 00:48:56.750
top picture, top
curve is for undoped.

00:48:56.750 --> 00:48:59.600
And you see different
doping concentrations.

00:48:59.600 --> 00:49:02.780
And you could see that
the resistance changes.

00:49:02.780 --> 00:49:06.170
And from our model,
we can predict

00:49:06.170 --> 00:49:09.940
what the effect of
carrier concentration is.

00:49:09.940 --> 00:49:12.920
We know how much
dopant we've added.

00:49:12.920 --> 00:49:15.870
We can imagine where
we are on this curve

00:49:15.870 --> 00:49:19.700
and then from that back
out what the temperature

00:49:19.700 --> 00:49:22.550
dependence of the mobility is.

00:49:22.550 --> 00:49:26.630
And the results here turned
out to be far better than I

00:49:26.630 --> 00:49:27.815
would ever have expected.

00:49:31.060 --> 00:49:36.150
We have a model here for
the contribution from

00:49:36.150 --> 00:49:40.020
electron-phonon interaction,
which is the undoped case,

00:49:40.020 --> 00:49:41.700
from defects--

00:49:41.700 --> 00:49:45.960
that's impurities that are
isoelectronic impurities that

00:49:45.960 --> 00:49:48.000
are present or other defects--

00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:51.120
and then charge scatterers.

00:49:51.120 --> 00:49:57.600
And using just this simple model
and the different temperature

00:49:57.600 --> 00:50:01.080
regimes, we're able to get
the correct temperature

00:50:01.080 --> 00:50:03.480
dependence for each of
these phenomenon, each

00:50:03.480 --> 00:50:05.110
of these processes.

00:50:05.110 --> 00:50:08.320
And so this was
beyond my expectation.

00:50:08.320 --> 00:50:11.190
I'm very proud of
Yumeng's result on this

00:50:11.190 --> 00:50:13.770
because this was
better than I had

00:50:13.770 --> 00:50:17.910
expected at our present
stage in this problem.

00:50:17.910 --> 00:50:23.410
The problem of doing the
p-type doping, p-type doping,

00:50:23.410 --> 00:50:30.360
is complicated by the
following physical phenomenon.

00:50:30.360 --> 00:50:34.320
Doping with p-type is
very much controlled

00:50:34.320 --> 00:50:38.670
by the p-type carriers that we
have in the intrinsic solid due

00:50:38.670 --> 00:50:40.830
to these T-point holes.

00:50:40.830 --> 00:50:44.010
And trying to lower
the Fermi level

00:50:44.010 --> 00:50:47.610
by adding p-type
impurities is greatly

00:50:47.610 --> 00:50:52.530
hampered by having this
large reservoir of holes.

00:50:52.530 --> 00:50:57.930
And what we're hoping to do is
a new optimization approach--

00:50:57.930 --> 00:51:00.210
this is work for the future--

00:51:00.210 --> 00:51:03.360
is by adding a small
amount of antimony,

00:51:03.360 --> 00:51:05.970
we hope to make some
kind of revolution

00:51:05.970 --> 00:51:08.010
on the band
structure of bismuth.

00:51:08.010 --> 00:51:12.480
We have a way of moving the
T-point holes, which are over

00:51:12.480 --> 00:51:16.500
here, to lower energies,
and try to get them out

00:51:16.500 --> 00:51:24.390
of the way of the carriers
that we would add by doping.

00:51:24.390 --> 00:51:27.990
We figure that a few
percent of antimony

00:51:27.990 --> 00:51:31.950
should be sufficient to
make a real difference

00:51:31.950 --> 00:51:38.250
and to get some good p-type
dopant for device applications

00:51:38.250 --> 00:51:41.040
where we need both n
and p-type materials.

00:51:44.630 --> 00:51:50.090
Finally, this project
is somewhat driven--

00:51:50.090 --> 00:51:52.880
we always have behind
us our masters who

00:51:52.880 --> 00:51:54.530
are supporting this research.

00:51:54.530 --> 00:51:59.540
And this is to try to make
a good thermoelectric.

00:51:59.540 --> 00:52:01.760
The metal insulator
transition-- that

00:52:01.760 --> 00:52:04.850
is the semimetal-semiconductor
transition--

00:52:04.850 --> 00:52:08.850
occurs at about 40
to 50 nanometers.

00:52:08.850 --> 00:52:10.510
That's a very, very big size.

00:52:10.510 --> 00:52:13.110
There's interesting
science to be done there,

00:52:13.110 --> 00:52:15.080
and we're working away
at that because that's

00:52:15.080 --> 00:52:17.720
necessary to go
to the next step.

00:52:17.720 --> 00:52:20.870
But our calculations
from our band models

00:52:20.870 --> 00:52:25.070
show us that the interesting
device applications occur

00:52:25.070 --> 00:52:28.190
at very much smaller sizes.

00:52:28.190 --> 00:52:31.580
To get ZT-- that is the
thermoelectric figure

00:52:31.580 --> 00:52:32.750
of merit--

00:52:32.750 --> 00:52:40.760
to be greater than 1, we have
to have our n-type samples to be

00:52:40.760 --> 00:52:43.440
on the order of 10 nanometers.

00:52:43.440 --> 00:52:45.230
So that's a big challenge.

00:52:45.230 --> 00:52:49.010
But even bigger challenge
is our p-type samples

00:52:49.010 --> 00:52:53.660
have to be smaller than about
5 nanometers unless we're smart

00:52:53.660 --> 00:52:58.460
and our idea with the
antimony doping works.

00:52:58.460 --> 00:53:01.520
So we have a hard
master here that's

00:53:01.520 --> 00:53:05.360
moving us to improve
our technology in making

00:53:05.360 --> 00:53:11.300
and characterizing our samples,
and forcing us to understand

00:53:11.300 --> 00:53:13.760
of our system in
great detail so we

00:53:13.760 --> 00:53:16.310
can make some
realistic predictions

00:53:16.310 --> 00:53:19.250
for this small size.

00:53:19.250 --> 00:53:21.860
In the meantime, we're
spending quite a bit of effort

00:53:21.860 --> 00:53:24.650
in trying to
understand the science

00:53:24.650 --> 00:53:28.190
of the semimetal-semiconductor
transition

00:53:28.190 --> 00:53:32.450
and in studying the properties
of this material, which

00:53:32.450 --> 00:53:35.000
in nature doesn't occur
as a semiconductor,

00:53:35.000 --> 00:53:38.270
but in low dimensions,
like nanowires, it does.

00:53:38.270 --> 00:53:41.600
And it's a remarkable
new kind of semiconductor

00:53:41.600 --> 00:53:45.710
that has very different features
from other semiconductors

00:53:45.710 --> 00:53:50.390
in terms of the carrier pockets,
their orientations, the very

00:53:50.390 --> 00:53:55.070
high mobilities that
are possible here,

00:53:55.070 --> 00:53:59.390
ballistic transport, and
many other phenomena.

00:53:59.390 --> 00:54:03.170
We're looking forward to having
some unusual optical properties

00:54:03.170 --> 00:54:05.870
because of the close coupling
between the conduction

00:54:05.870 --> 00:54:06.830
and valence band.

00:54:06.830 --> 00:54:10.280
We expect high
dielectric constants.

00:54:10.280 --> 00:54:13.430
And that might be
interesting for a whole band

00:54:13.430 --> 00:54:15.680
of other applications
because there

00:54:15.680 --> 00:54:18.350
aren't that many
semiconductors around with very

00:54:18.350 --> 00:54:20.600
high dielectric constants.

00:54:20.600 --> 00:54:26.270
So I will end my
talk at this point.

00:54:26.270 --> 00:54:29.990
We started a new area,
and a lot of people

00:54:29.990 --> 00:54:33.530
are looking at different
aspects of this in terms

00:54:33.530 --> 00:54:35.780
of nanostructural science.

00:54:35.780 --> 00:54:39.650
The fact that we have templates
has attracted a lot of people

00:54:39.650 --> 00:54:44.270
into template use for putting
a lot of other materials

00:54:44.270 --> 00:54:49.160
inside the pores, whether
they're magnetic materials

00:54:49.160 --> 00:54:52.600
or superconductors
or what have you.

00:54:52.600 --> 00:54:54.050
There are interesting
things to be

00:54:54.050 --> 00:54:57.800
done with this new
category of materials,

00:54:57.800 --> 00:55:02.810
and we continue also plugging
away at the thermoelectrics

00:55:02.810 --> 00:55:05.902
because those are our
masters at the moment.

00:55:05.902 --> 00:55:06.886
Thank you.

00:55:06.886 --> 00:55:10.330
[APPLAUSE]

00:55:14.770 --> 00:55:16.580
OK, do we have some questions?

00:55:16.580 --> 00:55:17.357
Yes?

00:55:17.357 --> 00:55:19.842
Have you observed
anything experimentally

00:55:19.842 --> 00:55:25.320
that you think you don't
understand theoretically?

00:55:25.320 --> 00:55:29.520
A lot of details are
not quite in place yet.

00:55:29.520 --> 00:55:34.140
And I would say
that, for example, I

00:55:34.140 --> 00:55:37.710
showed you the temperature
dependence of the resistivity.

00:55:37.710 --> 00:55:39.690
We can't fit
everything in detail,

00:55:39.690 --> 00:55:42.960
but we have the general
features blocked out now.

00:55:42.960 --> 00:55:45.120
And we have to add--

00:55:45.120 --> 00:55:49.320
well, for example, our
models are very crude.

00:55:49.320 --> 00:55:52.500
We have a constant relaxation
time approximation.

00:55:52.500 --> 00:55:56.580
We use that frequently
in doing our modeling.

00:55:56.580 --> 00:55:58.440
That obviously is
not sufficient.

00:55:58.440 --> 00:56:01.730
We should take into account
the actual scattering processes

00:56:01.730 --> 00:56:03.049
for phonons.

00:56:03.049 --> 00:56:07.540
There wasn't anything that seems
to be qualitatively peculiar.

00:56:07.540 --> 00:56:13.560
Not down to the sizes that we
have studied up to this point.

00:56:13.560 --> 00:56:17.160
I'm very interested-- there are
people at MIT that promise us

00:56:17.160 --> 00:56:20.910
that they could make us some
smaller nanowires, smaller

00:56:20.910 --> 00:56:24.660
than 4 nanometers in
diameter and smaller.

00:56:24.660 --> 00:56:25.890
I believe at some point--

00:56:25.890 --> 00:56:27.180
Who are they, by the way?

00:56:27.180 --> 00:56:27.740
Wendy--

00:56:27.740 --> 00:56:29.340
Oh, OK.

00:56:29.340 --> 00:56:32.800
--has a method, and
I hope it works.

00:56:32.800 --> 00:56:35.100
We don't know yet,
but that's one

00:56:35.100 --> 00:56:39.720
of the virtues of being in
a large research university

00:56:39.720 --> 00:56:42.000
with a lot of smart people.

00:56:42.000 --> 00:56:43.050
You get help.

00:56:43.050 --> 00:56:45.000
They listen to
what you're doing.

00:56:45.000 --> 00:56:47.610
They have ideas
about some aspect

00:56:47.610 --> 00:56:49.510
that interests them as well.

00:56:49.510 --> 00:56:53.190
And I believe that there's
going to be some diameter where

00:56:53.190 --> 00:56:56.310
we have a phase transition
and we don't have bismuth

00:56:56.310 --> 00:56:57.980
as we know it.

00:56:57.980 --> 00:57:02.100
It has a different crystal
structure from bulk bismuth.

00:57:02.100 --> 00:57:04.170
And then we're going
to have nanowires

00:57:04.170 --> 00:57:08.880
with some unknown properties,
maybe very interesting

00:57:08.880 --> 00:57:09.990
but certainly different.

00:57:09.990 --> 00:57:11.790
And we're looking
very hard to find out

00:57:11.790 --> 00:57:13.500
what happens in that regime.

00:57:13.500 --> 00:57:16.350
That's qualitatively different
from what we're doing now.

00:57:16.350 --> 00:57:18.880
And we have no idea
where that will occur.

00:57:18.880 --> 00:57:22.360
But we know down to 7
nanometers, it hasn't happened,

00:57:22.360 --> 00:57:24.810
because we have measurements
of transport properties

00:57:24.810 --> 00:57:27.210
down to 7 nanometers diameter.

00:57:27.210 --> 00:57:30.360
And that still is
like bulk bismuth.

00:57:30.360 --> 00:57:32.770
Now, bismuth has this
very long mean free path.

00:57:32.770 --> 00:57:36.180
I mean, how long is that
it at, say, 77 [? K. ?]

00:57:36.180 --> 00:57:37.880
It's millimeters long.

00:57:37.880 --> 00:57:39.670
And why is that?

00:57:39.670 --> 00:57:44.020
Why is it longer than, say, in a
modulation-doped semiconductor?

00:57:44.020 --> 00:57:45.460
Why is it longer than in a--

00:57:45.460 --> 00:57:48.310
Much longer, orders
of magnitude longer.

00:57:48.310 --> 00:57:51.970
Like, say in a usual--

00:57:51.970 --> 00:57:55.317
on a well, I mean, the mean
free paths are several microns,

00:57:55.317 --> 00:57:55.900
a few microns.

00:57:55.900 --> 00:57:59.110
OK, well, let me sort of
explain about bismuth.

00:57:59.110 --> 00:58:02.140
And lead telluride has some
of the same properties.

00:58:02.140 --> 00:58:05.360
If you have a material with a
very large dielectric constant,

00:58:05.360 --> 00:58:10.510
the carriers are sort of
self-shielded by the fact

00:58:10.510 --> 00:58:13.810
that you have a dielectric
constant of 100 or so.

00:58:13.810 --> 00:58:18.160
And I think that that
is a large part of--

00:58:18.160 --> 00:58:24.030
you have impurities present,
but the effect of the impurities

00:58:24.030 --> 00:58:26.430
is greatly reduced
by screening effects

00:58:26.430 --> 00:58:30.770
due to the high
dielectric constant.

00:58:30.770 --> 00:58:32.210
That's one answer to it.

00:58:32.210 --> 00:58:36.170
But of course, when
you have the quantum

00:58:36.170 --> 00:58:43.380
wires that we have here, the
mean free path decreases.

00:58:43.380 --> 00:58:44.076
It is--

00:58:44.076 --> 00:58:45.200
Compared to the bulk, yeah.

00:58:45.200 --> 00:58:47.871
Yeah, it's not larger than
the size of the nanowire.

00:58:47.871 --> 00:58:50.127
Yeah, OK.

00:58:50.127 --> 00:58:50.960
Any other questions?

00:58:50.960 --> 00:58:51.718
Yes?

00:58:51.718 --> 00:58:56.598
So I may have missed something,
your question earlier,

00:58:56.598 --> 00:58:58.306
because the reason
for the semiconductor

00:58:58.306 --> 00:59:00.520
is to get rid of the holes.

00:59:00.520 --> 00:59:02.740
The reason for going for
a semiconductor is we

00:59:02.740 --> 00:59:03.880
want a single carry.

00:59:03.880 --> 00:59:05.480
[INAUDIBLE] cancel
each other, right?

00:59:05.480 --> 00:59:06.820
Yeah, that's right.

00:59:06.820 --> 00:59:09.190
You have a whole range
of phenomena where

00:59:09.190 --> 00:59:11.140
electrons and holes cancels.

00:59:11.140 --> 00:59:12.940
[INAUDIBLE] effect
is one of them.

00:59:12.940 --> 00:59:14.781
Right.

00:59:14.781 --> 00:59:23.416
The reason I was asking
[INAUDIBLE] even regardless

00:59:23.416 --> 00:59:28.768
of what your doping
type is [INAUDIBLE]..

00:59:28.768 --> 00:59:30.980
The bandgap can get quite large.

00:59:30.980 --> 00:59:34.530
It can get more
than 100 millivolts.

00:59:34.530 --> 00:59:41.740
[INAUDIBLE]

00:59:41.740 --> 00:59:47.440
You could arrange the-- that's
what we're trying to do now,

00:59:47.440 --> 00:59:48.910
is to arrange--

00:59:48.910 --> 00:59:53.050
we would like to use
this as a 77 [? K. ?]

00:59:53.050 --> 00:59:54.970
For device applications.

00:59:54.970 --> 00:59:58.300
I think it fits bismuth
better than room temperature,

00:59:58.300 --> 01:00:01.360
although I think we probably
can make a device higher

01:00:01.360 --> 01:00:03.280
in temperature than 77.

01:00:03.280 --> 01:00:05.920
But the first thing that we're
trying to do is 77 [? K ?]

01:00:05.920 --> 01:00:10.750
because we know a lot about
the energy bands at 77 [? K. ?]

01:00:10.750 --> 01:00:12.280
At higher temperatures,
they start

01:00:12.280 --> 01:00:14.170
to get temperature-dependent.

01:00:14.170 --> 01:00:19.060
And the data for the
dependents of all parameters

01:00:19.060 --> 01:00:20.690
on temperature is very poor.

01:00:20.690 --> 01:00:23.710
So we have a lot of
research to be done just

01:00:23.710 --> 01:00:29.550
to find out what people normally
know about semiconductors.

01:00:29.550 --> 01:00:31.590
And this-- yes, Caroline.

01:00:31.590 --> 01:00:34.701
I know some people
who [INAUDIBLE]..

01:00:34.701 --> 01:00:35.200
Yes.

01:00:35.200 --> 01:00:40.100
What kind of difference is
in the physical structures of

01:00:40.100 --> 01:00:42.060
[INAUDIBLE]?

01:00:42.060 --> 01:00:46.890
OK, electrodeposition,
electrodeposition

01:00:46.890 --> 01:00:50.700
is one of the things
that we hope to try.

01:00:50.700 --> 01:00:53.550
Electrodeposition
has certain virtues

01:00:53.550 --> 01:00:57.330
in that the wire at the
beginning and the end

01:00:57.330 --> 01:01:01.740
is defined so that you can
make contact to all wires

01:01:01.740 --> 01:01:02.560
that you have.

01:01:02.560 --> 01:01:07.200
So that's a very big plus.

01:01:07.200 --> 01:01:09.450
Up until now, there
has been some work

01:01:09.450 --> 01:01:13.650
done on elctrodeposition
bismuth nanowires.

01:01:13.650 --> 01:01:17.970
The problem is that the mean
free paths of those nanowires

01:01:17.970 --> 01:01:22.170
is rather short and they can not
see ballistic transport the way

01:01:22.170 --> 01:01:25.230
we do it with our nanowires.

01:01:25.230 --> 01:01:28.650
That is, they have many,
many grains along the length

01:01:28.650 --> 01:01:29.930
of a single nanowire.

01:01:29.930 --> 01:01:33.570
We have relatively few grades.

01:01:33.570 --> 01:01:36.960
We have some ideas
about how to make--

01:01:36.960 --> 01:01:41.530
once we get a nanowire
in our template,

01:01:41.530 --> 01:01:46.330
we have ideas how we can
make the grade size very much

01:01:46.330 --> 01:01:47.080
larger.

01:01:47.080 --> 01:01:57.020
So it's similar to what we have
in the pressure preparation.

01:01:57.020 --> 01:01:59.410
This is only up here
with me and my students.

01:01:59.410 --> 01:02:00.730
It's at the talking stage.

01:02:00.730 --> 01:02:03.950
But I hope that
within a year or so,

01:02:03.950 --> 01:02:07.730
we'll be able to demonstrate
some of these concepts.

01:02:07.730 --> 01:02:13.370
The other thing about the
electrochemical method,

01:02:13.370 --> 01:02:15.860
we hope to be able
to combine that

01:02:15.860 --> 01:02:21.120
with orienting the nanowires
along desired directions.

01:02:21.120 --> 01:02:22.760
So we could pick
the best direction,

01:02:22.760 --> 01:02:28.270
which is the trigonal direction
for electrons and whatever

01:02:28.270 --> 01:02:28.780
direction--

01:02:28.780 --> 01:02:30.250
I've forgotten-- for the holes.

01:02:30.250 --> 01:02:35.680
That will depend on the
dopant scheme that we use.

01:02:35.680 --> 01:02:36.990
But that's the concept.

01:02:36.990 --> 01:02:42.960
So we could use the substrate
to orient the wires.

01:02:42.960 --> 01:02:44.250
But that's to be demonstrated.

01:02:44.250 --> 01:02:45.984
I'm not sure we'll succeed.

01:02:45.984 --> 01:02:46.650
Do I understand?

01:02:46.650 --> 01:02:48.300
In this thermoelectric
application,

01:02:48.300 --> 01:02:50.520
you'd have some sort of
an array of nanowires?

01:02:50.520 --> 01:02:51.070
Yeah, yeah.

01:02:51.070 --> 01:02:52.570
And you'd pass
current to them and--

01:02:52.570 --> 01:02:58.410
Yeah, for thermoelectric,
if you're designing a wafer,

01:02:58.410 --> 01:03:02.820
you have some integrated
circuit and you put this thing

01:03:02.820 --> 01:03:04.260
underneath.

01:03:04.260 --> 01:03:08.100
The geometry is what
they would like.

01:03:08.100 --> 01:03:10.680
They don't really want
the geometry to be planar.

01:03:10.680 --> 01:03:12.810
They like to have
the heat moving away

01:03:12.810 --> 01:03:16.470
from the plane of the
integrated circuits.

01:03:16.470 --> 01:03:20.510
So this is desirable.

01:03:20.510 --> 01:03:23.255
And we can make these--

01:03:23.255 --> 01:03:24.130
well, they could be--

01:03:24.130 --> 01:03:26.524
I was thinking more of
replacing my refrigerator, but--

01:03:26.524 --> 01:03:27.940
Oh, your refrigerator,
well, you'd

01:03:27.940 --> 01:03:29.370
just have to have
a lot of these.

01:03:29.370 --> 01:03:30.730
I see.

01:03:30.730 --> 01:03:31.498
Question for you.

01:03:31.498 --> 01:03:35.800
What's the Fermi
wavelength in these wires?

01:03:35.800 --> 01:03:38.220
The Fermi wavelength
is pretty short

01:03:38.220 --> 01:03:41.980
because your effective
masses are small.

01:03:41.980 --> 01:03:46.470
Fermi surfaces are tiny compared
to the [INAUDIBLE] dimensions.

01:03:46.470 --> 01:03:51.448
So your Fermi
wavelength is small.

01:03:51.448 --> 01:03:53.440
If you compare it with
the wide diameter,

01:03:53.440 --> 01:03:55.440
is it bigger or smaller?

01:03:55.440 --> 01:03:59.590
You could arrange it
to be either way by--

01:03:59.590 --> 01:04:05.560
I believe you could adjust the
design with your Fermi level

01:04:05.560 --> 01:04:08.590
to be in either limit.

01:04:08.590 --> 01:04:11.140
We haven't worked
on that problem yet,

01:04:11.140 --> 01:04:14.430
but I believe you can do either
one, both for n and p-type,

01:04:14.430 --> 01:04:15.908
for that matter.

01:04:15.908 --> 01:04:17.980
So if it's greater
than the wide diameter,

01:04:17.980 --> 01:04:20.070
the wire should be
one-dimensional.

01:04:20.070 --> 01:04:23.040
And if it's smaller, it
should be three-dimensional.

01:04:23.040 --> 01:04:25.960
Yeah well, we see those
kinds of transitions already.

01:04:29.190 --> 01:04:33.405
Almost all the work up until
now on sort of classical size

01:04:33.405 --> 01:04:37.620
effect have been on
three-dimensional limit.

01:04:37.620 --> 01:04:40.320
And this work that we've
done is the first work

01:04:40.320 --> 01:04:43.690
in the one-dimensional.

01:04:43.690 --> 01:04:48.250
You need to have a small wire
to do the one-dimensional case.

01:04:48.250 --> 01:04:50.740
OK, with no more
questions, I think

01:04:50.740 --> 01:04:51.940
we should thank our speaker.

01:04:51.940 --> 01:04:55.590
[APPLAUSE]

